This is also already applied. So the current code has all these fixes. The issue 42488123 is a specific heap-buffer-overflow READ 1. Let me look at the current code more carefully to find the remaining bug.

Let me look at the logfmt parser's value parsing. In the current code:

```c
if (*c == '=') {
    c++;
    if (c < end) {
        if (*c == '"') {
            c++;
            value = c;
            value_str = FLB_TRUE;
            while (c < end) {
                if (*c != '\\' && *c!= '"') {
                    c++;
                }
                else if (*c == '\\') {
                    value_escape =  FLB_TRUE;
                    c++;
                    if (c == end) {
                        break;
                    }
                    c++;
                }
                else {
                    break;
                }
            }
            value_len = c - value;
            if (c < end && *c == '\"') {
                c++;
            }
        }
        ...
```

This looks fine. Let me think about the typecast BOOL issue. The fuzzer sets up types with "CCC" being BOOL. When the parser encounters a key "CCC" with a short value, `strncasecmp(val, "true", 4)` reads 4 bytes. If the value is at the end of the buffer and short, this reads past the heap buffer.

Actually, let me reconsider. The heap-buffer-overflow READ 1 means reading 1 byte past the end. `strncasecmp` reads byte by byte until it finds a difference or a null byte. If the value is e.g. "t" (1 byte) at the end of the buffer, then `strncasecmp(val, "true", 4)` reads 't' (matches), then reads the next byte which is past the buffer → READ 1 overflow.

But wait, the input buffer passed to `flb_parser_do` is `(char*)data` with `size`. The data is the fuzz input. The value `val` points into this buffer. So if the value is at the very end of the buffer, reading past it is a heap-buffer-overflow.

Let me verify this is the actual bug. Let me look at the fix for this specific issue. Let me search the git history for the typecast BOOL fix.

